{
  "cells": [
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "# Conditional GANs & Pix2Pix Notebook\n\n> Hands-on Build It and Exercises."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "## Build It"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "`code/main.py` implements a tiny conditional GAN on 1-D data. The condition `c` is a class label (0 or 1). The task: produce a sample from the conditional distribution for the given class."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "### Step 1: append condition to both G and D inputs"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": [
        "```python"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "def G(z, c, params):"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "    return mlp(concat([z, one_hot(c)]), params)"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "def D(x, c, params):"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "    return mlp(concat([x, one_hot(c)]), params)"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": [
        "```"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "One-hot encoding is the simplest way. Larger models use learned embeddings, FiLM modulation, or cross-attention."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "### Step 2: train conditional"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": [
        "```python"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "for step in range(steps):"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "    x, c = sample_real_conditional()"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "    noise = sample_noise()"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "    update_D(x_real=x, x_fake=G(noise, c), c=c)"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "    update_G(noise, c)"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": [
        "```"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "The generator must match the real distribution *for the given condition*, not the marginal."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "### Step 3: verify per-class output"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": [
        "```python"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "for c in [0, 1]:"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "    samples = [G(noise, c) for noise in batch]"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "    mean_c = mean(samples)"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "    assert_near(mean_c, real_mean_for_class_c)"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": [
        "```"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "## Exercises"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": [
        "1. **Easy.** Modify `code/main.py` to add a third class. Confirm G still maps each class's noise to the correct mode.\n2. **Medium.** Replace L1 with a perceptual-style loss in the 1-D setting (e.g. a small frozen D acting as feature extractor). Does it change sharpness of the conditional distribution?\n3. **Hard.** Sketch a CycleGAN in the 1-D setting: two distributions, two generators, cycle loss. Show that it learns to map between them with no paired data."
      ]
    }
